<Base title="Email standards map | howto.email" description="A practical map of the major RFCs and standards behind modern Internet email."><div class="wrap page article"><p class="kicker">STANDARDS</p><h1>Email standards map</h1><p>Email is a stack of standards accumulated over decades. This map points to the parts most operators encounter. For a larger catalog, see the <a href="/reference/rfc-index/">RFC index</a> and <a href="/reference/rfc-map/">RFC map</a>.</p>
<h2>Core message and transport</h2><p><strong>RFC 5321</strong> defines SMTP transport. <strong>RFC 5322</strong> defines Internet Message Format. <strong>RFC 6409</strong> covers message submission. Start with <a href="/reference/smtp/">SMTP</a> and <a href="/reference/message-format/">message format</a>.</p>
<h2>Message bodies and attachments</h2><p>The MIME family defines media types, multipart bodies, transfer encodings, and non-ASCII content. See <a href="/reference/mime/">MIME</a>, <a href="/reference/content-type/">Content-Type</a>, and <a href="/reference/content-transfer-encoding/">Content-Transfer-Encoding</a>.</p>
<h2>Authentication</h2><p><strong>RFC 7208</strong> defines SPF. <strong>RFC 6376</strong> defines DKIM. DMARC's current standards-track specification is covered in <a href="/reference/dmarc-current-standard/">the current DMARC standard</a>. See also <a href="/learn/email-authentication/">email authentication</a>.</p>
<h2>Transport security</h2><p><strong>RFC 3207</strong> defines SMTP STARTTLS, <strong>RFC 8461</strong> defines MTA-STS, <strong>RFC 8460</strong> defines TLS-RPT, and <strong>RFC 7672</strong> defines SMTP security using DANE. See <a href="/reference/starttls/">STARTTLS</a>, <a href="/reference/mta-sts/">MTA-STS</a>, <a href="/reference/tls-rpt/">TLS-RPT</a>, and <a href="/reference/dane-smtp/">DANE</a>.</p>
<h2>Delivery status and feedback</h2><p>Enhanced status codes and DSNs make delivery failures machine-readable; feedback-report formats support abuse reporting. See <a href="/reference/enhanced-status-codes/">enhanced status codes</a>, <a href="/reference/dsn/">DSN</a>, and <a href="/reference/arf/">ARF</a>.</p>
<h2>Lists and unsubscribe</h2><p>List headers and one-click unsubscribe have their own standards and operational requirements. See <a href="/reference/list-headers/">List-* headers</a> and <a href="/reference/one-click-unsubscribe/">one-click unsubscribe</a>.</p>
<h2>Reading standards safely</h2><p>An RFC number alone does not tell you whether a document is current, updated, experimental, informational, or superseded. Check the RFC Editor record and errata before treating an older document as the final word.</p>
<h2>Reference material</h2><ul><li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/">RFC Editor</a></li><li><a href="https://www.iana.org/protocols">IANA protocol registries</a></li></ul></div></Base>